375 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 176 0 R
/E (Keywords: Named Entity Recognition, Character Extraction, Co-occurrence Analysis, Graph-Based Literary Analysis, Sentiment Scoring, Large Language Model, Gemini API, Digital Humanities)
/Pg 17 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
376 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 176 0 R
/E (On Dracula by Bram Stoker, the system achieved an F1-score of 93.4% for character extraction when running with Gemini, compared to 72.4% for raw spaCy without any post-processing. The offline-only mode \(no API key needed\) hit 89% F1. Processing a full novel takes roughly 15-30 seconds depending on the size.)
/Pg 17 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
377 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 176 0 R
/E (Reading a long novel carefully enough to track every character and map out how they relate to each other is genuinely hard work. It takes hours, sometimes days, and even then a reader can miss subtle patterns - characters who barely interact directly but are constantly mentioned together, or how a relationship that seems friendly in the early chapters turns hostile by the end. LitMind was built to handle this kind of analysis automatically.)
/Pg 17 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
378 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 176 0 R
/E (The frontend is a Streamlit web app with three tabs. The first shows the character network graph and a sortable table of centrality scores. The second shows Gemini's summary of the plot, genre classification, tone, and setting. The third shows book recommendations based on the user's reading history.)
/Pg 17 0 R
/K [6]
>>
endobj
379 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 176 0 R
/E (The system takes a novel or play as plain text input and runs it through a pipeline that does three things: first, it finds all the named characters using spaCy's Named Entity Recognition and then cleans up the mess of duplicate names and aliases that any real novel produces. Second, it figures out how often pairs of characters appear near each other in the text, builds a weighted co-occurrence graph using NetworkX, and computes centrality scores to identify the characters who are most connected to the overall story. Third and this is the part that requires actual semantic understanding it calls Google's Gemini API to classify the type of relationship between the most prominent character pairs and assign a sentiment score between -1.0 \(deeply hostile\) and +1.0 \(strongly allied\).)
/Pg 17 0 R
/K [7]
>>
endobj
380 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H1
/P 177 0 R
/T (1. INTRODUCTION)
/E (1. INTRODUCTION)
/Pg 18 0 R
/K [0]
>>
endobj
381 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 177 0 R
/E (Literary Analysis is a demanding mental pursuit it involves annotation, reading and expert critical analysis. While close reading can untangle a head of Literature into an artichoke of themes, it provides far-flung assistance to balanced comparisons of hundreds or hundreds of thousands of works, performs, or archive containers of early editions in manuscript. Now, that it is posted and obtainable to the everyone through efforts like Venture Gutenberg \(PG\) and other repository sites, Digital Humanities has turned to pcs to define structures inside of Literature.)
/Pg 18 0 R
/K [1]
>>
endobj
382 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 177 0 R
/E (I think we began with the notion of our project Lit Mind as a concrete thing that takes what is merely raw novel text and turns it into a valuable knowledge. We talked about what type of a small project should definitely be considered valuable rather than just academically perfect. We felt that having to think about the difficulty of tracking characters and their relationships should be interesting but possible within the span of a semester.)
/Pg 18 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
383 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 177 0 R
/E <4C69744D696E642050726F6A656374205265706F7274208420312E20496E74726F64756374696F6E>
/Pg 18 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
384 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 177 0 R
/E (Page 9)
/Pg 18 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
385 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H2
/P 177 0 R
/T (1.2 Project Scope and Objectives)
/E (1.2 Project Scope and Objectives)
/Pg 18 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
386 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 177 0 R
/E <>
/Pg 18 0 R
/K [6]
>>
endobj
387 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 177 0 R
/E (However, while we were testing other tools, we came to the conclusion that almost all of them required users to annotate beforehand or they just worked really poorly on newsworthy literature with even a few paragraphs at all. There was not one easy-to-use, browserbased app that would take a version of the text from Dracula in the form of a Dracula.txt file and immediately identify the key players, their interactions with each other, and what all of these interactions meant. This is Lit Mind.)
/Pg 18 0 R
/K [7]
>>
endobj
388 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 177 0 R
/E (Another reason was the practicality of learning. Working on this project has given us insights into real NLP pipelines, API's and data visualisation which are all important skills for modern software developers. It was also an interesting chance to do some real-world graph theory, which we had only studied from a theoretical perspective in our algorithms class.)
/Pg 18 0 R
/K [8]
>>
endobj
389 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 177 0 R
/E (Lit Mind is a conjunction between latest Generation AI and conventional graph theory. It is an interactive, web-based platform for students, teachers, and literary researchers. The platform takes as input raw text \(.txt. Pdf\) files of plays and novels, and produces the data dashboards. Lit Mind automates six major analyses of the novel/play on the basis of:)
/Pg 18 0 R
/K [9]
>>
endobj
390 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 177 0 R
/E (Automatically extracting and de-duplicating entire character rosters from new text. Network centrality to mathematically distinguish primary lead characters from major supporting characters.)
/Pg 18 0 R
/K [10]
>>
endobj
391 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H2
/P 177 0 R
/T (1.1 Motivation)
/E (1.1 Motivation)
/Pg 18 0 R
/K [11]
>>
endobj
392 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 177 0 R
/E (Inter-character relationship types \(Ally, Enemy, Family, Romantic\) categorization qualitatively. Quantitative relationship scoring by Gemini LLM reasoning with NumPy numerical aggregation. Tons of executive summaries put together based on map-reduce prompting approach. Multi- dimensional theme and genre categories.)
/Pg 18 0 R
/K [12]
>>
endobj
