/E (Unique to the literary domain, however, is alias resolution \(consolidating every description of a character with a single token\) Vala et al. [4] tackled this and argued that an off-the-shelf NER results in dozens of fictitious character 'entities' per novel, since every surface form is handled independently. In their Victorian novel corpus, they showed how a system lacking alias resolution will yield between 150 and 200 names for Dracula alone, most of which are reduced to a handful of synonyms or derivatives for the same 10-12 characters.)
/Pg 21 0 R
/K [28]
>>
endobj
444 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 180 0 R
/E (A more recent paper by Labatut and Bost [4] gave a survey of the various strategies for tackling this problem. They divided the various approaches into three families: co- occurrence networks \(characters connected because they're near each other in the text\), conversational networks \(characters connected because they speak to each other\), and action networks \(characters connected because they act upon each other\). The three families of techniques each have their own relative advantages and disadvantages for alias resolution; for Lit Mind we've decided to use a co- occurrence technique with a custom alias resolution pipeline that uses a mix of title stripping, substring containment matching, and \(optionally\) Gemini review for ambiguous cases.)
/Pg 21 0 R
/K [29]
>>
endobj
