497 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 185 0 R
/E (The now character extraction module is the most technical part of the pipeline. It always receives the original input text and runs spaCy NER on the entire text in chunks of 100K characters \(overlap of 2K\). We need to do this both for spaCy \(maximum document length is limited\) and for memory reasons.)
/Pg 26 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
498 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 185 0 R
/E (The initial one we describe here is the label-voting filter. For each unique surface-form name extracted by NER, the module counts for the entire book how many times SPAcy assigns the label GPE, ORG or LOC rather than PERSON. Name is retained as a character candidate if the label PERSON received the plurality vote. For example, Dracula receives the correct elimination of Exeter because it was assigned by spaCy as GPE more often than as PERSON.)
/Pg 26 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
499 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 185 0 R
/E (Third is the dependency filter. Each mention appears in context around characters, which are the players of each action - the subject and object of the verb. Most place name mentions tend to be objects of in, near or at. Our module looks at the dependency parse for each mention, and removes all those which are very often occurring in that context.)
/Pg 26 0 R
/K [6]
>>
endobj
500 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 185 0 R
/E (The text preprocessing module accepts the input file and outputs a cleaned, normalized, string of text. \(Some may think this step trivial, but it involves some work in order to guarantee a higher quality of NLP further down the pipeline. Our main source of texts - Project Gutenberg - displays odd behaviors which we must resolve\).)
/Pg 26 0 R
/K [7]
>>
endobj
501 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 185 0 R
/E (For.txt files, the module tries those encodings \(in that sequence\) for each, and returns the first that succeed, resorting to a replacement-character decode if all of them fail. This is important as many of the older Gutenberg files were encoded in Latin-1 \(CP1252\) rather than UTF-8. For.pdf files, the text layer is obtained via the getPageText\(\) method of the PyMuPDF library, one page at a time.)
/Pg 26 0 R
/K [8]
>>
endobj
502 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 185 0 R
/E (The normalization pipeline works on the documents in this order: Normalize all line endings to use the UNIX line ending \\n. Remove Project Gutenberg license header and footer via regexes. Remove hard- line break encodings, so Hel-\\nsing has the same meaning as Helsing. Rejoin hard-wrapped lines in paragraphs \(removing the line break\). \(Removing the paragraph does not change the paragraph structure, so paragraph tokens can be used later to join up the lines \(span boundaries\) in sentences.\) However, spaCy's sentence detector does not like hard wrapped lines, as it expects all formatting to be normal prose punctuation. If this step is not included, spaCy will generate a sentence at the end of every wrapped line, breaking NER and co-occurrence.)
/Pg 26 0 R
/K [9]
>>
endobj
