/E (7.6 NetworkX Graph Library)
/Pg 34 0 R
/K [6]
>>
endobj
718 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 193 0 R
/E (Networkx versionused for constructing and processing the character interaction graph:3.2+. An undirectedweightedgraph, where each node is a character, each two co-occurring characters are associated with one un-directededge with the normalized strength score as edge weight. The positions of all nodes on the 2D plane are generated by using spring_layout.)
/Pg 34 0 R
/K [7]
>>
endobj
719 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 193 0 R
/E (Three metrics are given for each network: degree centrality \(the number of edges / max number of possible edges\), betweenness centrality \(the number of shortest paths through a node / total number of shortest paths\) and weighted degree \(the sum of the weights of the edges\). These are presented in a sortable table on the Streamlit page.)
/Pg 34 0 R
/K [8]
>>
endobj
720 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H1
/P 193 0 R
/T (7.8 Streamlit Web Framework)
/E (7.8 Streamlit Web Framework)
/Pg 34 0 R
/K [9]
>>
endobj
721 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 193 0 R
/E (The web UI has 3 tabs: Character analysis and Show character table \(Alias list, mentions, gender, optional Gemini role\) and the rejected-candidates, Plotly network graph Book summary and Show Gemini summarized plot, genre, themes, tone and setting Reading taste match and Show the recommendation model metrics, and the top-20 books recommendation.)
/Pg 34 0 R
/K [10]
>>
endobj
722 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 193 0 R
/E (The output of the analysis is stored in streamlit's session state \(st.session_state\), so that we can access it from the user input interaction. If we don't do this the expensive NLP pipeline would be run every time the user interacts with anything in the UI. In the session state, we store the Character Result, list of Relations, and Book Analysis Report objects.)
/Pg 34 0 R
/K [11]
>>
endobj
723 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H1
/P 193 0 R
/T (7.5 API Authentication and Integration Logic)
/E (7.5 API Authentication and Integration Logic)
/Pg 34 0 R
/K [12]
>>
endobj
724 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 193 0 R
/E ( )
/Pg 34 0 R
/K [13]
>>
endobj
725 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 193 0 R
/E (APIqueries toGoogle GenerativeAI are completely authenticated \(authorization via environment variable GEMINIAPIKEY\). We use Gemini responsemimetype="application/json" configuration parameter and Pydantic schema validation to be sure that input requests always return deterministic \(parses without error\) schemas.)
/Pg 34 0 R
/K [14]
>>
endobj
726 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 193 0 R
/E (API exceptions in a try/except block and display those to the Streamlit app for the user. This means that an API outage does not crash our app and our end user gets an informative error message.)
/Pg 34 0 R
/K [15]
>>
endobj
727 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 193 0 R
/E (Google-generativeaiPython SDK\(0.8.3+\) used to interact with the Gemini API All API keys are stored as environment variables and are retrieved using python-dotenv - they are never committed into source code files or version control.)
/Pg 34 0 R
/K [16]
>>
endobj
728 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 193 0 R
/E (Most challenging engineering problem was API request limit - For long novels \(which requires multiple API calls \(refer to map-reduce strategy to summarization\)\) - the tracker requests to the API multiple times in sequence, and the quota of the number of requests per minute is exceeded. This is tackled by the Gemini client module through retries with exponential backoff.)
/Pg 34 0 R
/K [17]
>>
endobj
