970 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 200 0 R
/E (Fifth - Evaluation Corpus Limitation The single largest limitation we believe we need to be transparent about is the absence of a human annotation physical evaluation corpus in our current setup for all literary NLP tasks. An ideal evaluation of system such as Lit Mind would be to compare its output to a ground-truth dataset of human annotation who had read the source novels, annotated all character entities, grouping of character aliases, and the type of relationship between all character pairs. As far as we are aware, such a corpus is not publicly available in the exact task definition multi-novel, multi-character, and relationship- sentiment labels.)
/Pg 41 0 R
/K [8]
>>
endobj
971 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 200 0 R
/E (For now, to give you a more usable answer, we generated an artificially intelligent evaluation corpus with an AI assisted annotation workflow - we had Gemini 1.5 Pro produce reference annotations for a sample of Dracula and Sherlock Holmes, we revised them ourselves, and we used those revised annotations as our groundtruth. This allows us to determine our precision, recall, and F1 score for the results in Chapter 8 - however, we acknowledge that AI created groundtruth has its own biases, and is definitely not a substitute for a human annotation.)
/Pg 41 0 R
/K [9]
>>
endobj
972 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 200 0 R
/E (We are working with our supervisor, Dr. Sreemoyee Biswas, on creating a proper physical evaluation corpus. We would like to re-run all the evaluations against this corpus and human-annotated ground truth in the future, and update the metrics in this report. Therefore the metrics in this report should be considered estimates, rather than benchmarks.)
/Pg 41 0 R
/K [10]
>>
endobj
